issues.problematic_code_snippet:
/* This function is called at server startup in order to initialize cluster data
 * structures that are shared between the different cluster implementations. */
void clusterCommonInit(void) {
    server.cluster_slot_stats = malloc(CLUSTER_SLOTS*sizeof(clusterSlotStat));
    resetClusterStats();
    asmInit();
}

/* Delete keys in unowned slots */
clusterDeleteKeysInUnownedSlots();
/* Take over slots that we have keys for, but are assigned to no one. */
clusterClaimUnassignedSlots();
return C_OK;

void clusterDeleteKeysInUnownedSlots(void) {
    if (clusterNodeIsSlave(getMyClusterNode())) return;

    /* Check that all the slots we have keys for are assigned to us. Otherwise,
     * delete the keys. */

void clusterDeleteKeysInUnownedSlots(void) {
    if (clusterNodeIsSlave(getMyClusterNode())) return;

    /* Check that all the slots we have keys for are assigned to us. Otherwise,
     * delete the keys. */
    for (int i = 0; i < CLUSTER_SLOTS; i++) {
        /* Skip if: no keys in the slot, it's our slot, or we are importing it. */
        if (!countKeysInSlot(i) ||
            clusterIsMySlot(i) ||
            getImportingSlotSource(i))
        {
            continue;
        }
        ...
        clusterDelKeysInSlot(i, 0);
    }
}

findings.body:
The new `clusterDeleteKeysInUnownedSlots()` skips a slot only when it is mine (`clusterIsMySlot`) or being imported. A slot with no owner (`getNodeBySlot(i) == NULL`) is neither, so it is not skipped. Keys in such a slot are then deleted with `clusterDelKeysInSlot(i, 0)`.

It runs before `clusterClaimUnassignedSlots()` in `verifyClusterConfigWithData()`. By the time the claim pass runs, the keys are gone, and the slot is never adopted. The original code checked for `slots[j] == NULL` first and claimed the slot. The new order reverses that, so a restarting node drops data it used to keep.

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2146-2168"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The PR says there are no behavior changes. This one is a data-loss change, and no test exercises the startup path to catch it.

The fix is to run the claim pass first. Once a slot is claimed it counts as mine, so the delete pass skips it. Suggested order in `verifyClusterConfigWithData()`:

::suggestion{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2209-2212"}
    /* Take over slots that we have keys for but no one owns. This must run
     * before the delete pass, which would otherwise drop those keys. */
    clusterClaimUnassignedSlots();
    /* Delete keys in slots owned by other nodes. */
    clusterDeleteKeysInUnownedSlots();

`clusterCommonInit()` now allocates `server.cluster_slot_stats` with plain `malloc`. The previous code in `main()` used `zmalloc`. That causes two problems.

First, memory accounting changes. `zmalloc` tracks allocations for `used_memory` and related INFO fields, so the slot-stats array, roughly `CLUSTER_SLOTS * sizeof(clusterSlotStat)` bytes, is now missing from those figures.

Second, the return value is never checked. If `malloc` fails, `resetClusterStats()` passes NULL to `memset` in `clusterSlotStatResetAll()` and the server crashes. `zmalloc` aborts with a clear OOM message instead. Nothing in the codebase frees this buffer, so the allocator mismatch has no direct cost, but the accounting and failure-mode change are unintended.

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2135-2141"}

::callout{type="info"}
The PR description says there are no behavioral changes. Switching to `malloc` is one.

Nothing under `tests/` checks the startup path in `verifyClusterConfigWithData()`. A grep for the log strings "unassigned slot", "Taking responsibility" and "assigned to another node" finds no matches. That is why the ordering regression in this PR went unnoticed.

A test should start a cluster node with keys in a slot that has no owner and check two things: the keys survive the restart, and the node takes ownership of the slot. A second case should cover keys in a slot owned by another node, where the keys must be deleted. Both cases need to run before the refactor lands and must pass after the fix.

::code{file="src/cluster_legacy.c" lines="5262-5281"}

Previously `asmCron()` ran at the end of `clusterCron()`. It now runs before `clusterCron()` in the same `serverCron` tick. Both still run every 100ms, but any ASM logic that reads cluster state updated by `clusterCron()`, such as `clusterUpdateState()`, now sees the previous tick's state.

I could not find a concrete failure from reading the code, so this is a low-severity observation. The author should confirm that the new order is intended and that ASM cron does not depend on state that `clusterCron()` updates.

